====================================================
FINDING 2: Lack of Effective Application-Layer Rate Limiting Enabling Repeated Email Generation
====================================================

DESCRIPTION:
Once a valid card number is identified, repeated submissions of that same card number to the verify endpoint each trigger a new outbound email to the cardholder's associated address, with no observed per-card or per-session cap on the application layer during testing. During the internal assessment, over two thousand emails were generated against a single valid card number without any service disruption, demonstrating the absence of an application-enforced limit on this action. This was only reproducible because testing traffic was exempted from WAF inspection via an internal allowlist rule; production traffic is subject to F5 protection with default rate limiting, and access still requires a valid, time-bound bearer token obtained via client credentials. The endpoint combines a resource-consumption weakness at the application layer with compensating network-layer controls not present in the tester's vantage point during this specific test.

ATTACK AND RISK SCENARIO:
An attacker with valid API credentials and a known-valid card number (potentially obtained via Finding 1) could repeatedly invoke the verify endpoint to flood the associated cardholder's inbox, causing a nuisance/harassment condition and potentially masking legitimate transaction notification emails. Under real-world conditions, F5's default rate limiting on this endpoint is expected to cap the achievable request volume well below what was observed during the uninspected internal test, reducing both the scale of email flooding and the likelihood of the attack going undetected.

RECOMMENDATION:
- Implement an explicit application-layer cap (per-card and per-client-credential cooldown) on resend/verify actions, independent of network-layer (F5) rate limiting.
- Alert on repeated verify requests targeting the same card number from a single API client within a short window.
- Periodically validate that F5 rate-limiting rules for this endpoint remain active and correctly scoped.

